### Variations

**Variation 1: File-Based Configuration (Development Only)**

**When to Use**: Local development environments, unit testing, rapid prototyping

**Approach**: Store extension configuration in JSON files per tenant:

```
config/
  extensions/
    fl-medicaid/
      fl-prior-auth.json
      fl-claim-edits.json
    ca-medicaid/
      ca-medi-cal-rules.json
```

```csharp
public class FileBasedExtensionConfigurationProvider : IExtensionConfigurationProvider
{
    private readonly string _basePath;

    public async Task<T> GetConfigurationAsync<T>(
        string tenantId,
        string extensionId) where T : class, new()
    {
        var path = Path.Combine(_basePath, tenantId, $"{extensionId}.json");
        var json = await File.ReadAllTextAsync(path);
        return JsonSerializer.Deserialize<T>(json);
    }
}
```

**Trade-offs**:
- ✅ Simple implementation for development
- ✅ Configuration visible in source control
- ✅ Easy to edit and test
- ❌ Not suitable for production (no encryption, no audit trail)
- ❌ Secrets stored in plain text
- ❌ No dynamic updates without restart

**Variation 2: Azure App Configuration Integration**

**When to Use**: Cloud-native deployments, need for dynamic configuration updates, feature flag integration

**Approach**: Store extension configuration in Azure App Configuration:

```csharp
public class AzureAppConfigExtensionProvider : IExtensionConfigurationProvider
{
    private readonly IConfigurationRefresher _refresher;
    private readonly IConfiguration _configuration;

    public async Task<T> GetConfigurationAsync<T>(
        string tenantId,
        string extensionId) where T : class, new()
    {
        // Configuration key: Extensions:{tenantId}:{extensionId}
        var sectionKey = $"Extensions:{tenantId}:{extensionId}";

        // Trigger refresh if needed
        await _refresher.TryRefreshAsync();

        // Bind configuration section to type
        var config = new T();
        _configuration.GetSection(sectionKey).Bind(config);

        return config;
    }
}

// Startup configuration
services.AddAzureAppConfiguration();
services.Configure<AzureAppConfigurationOptions>(options =>
{
    options.ConfigurationRefreshInterval = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1);
    options.Watch("Extensions:*");  // Watch all extension config
});
```

**Trade-offs**:
- ✅ Centralized configuration management
- ✅ Dynamic refresh without restart
- ✅ Integration with feature flags
- ✅ Audit trail and versioning built-in
- ✅ Integration with Key Vault for secrets
- ❌ Additional Azure service cost
- ❌ Network dependency for configuration access

**Variation 3: Database-Stored JSON (Platform Standard)**

**When to Use**: Self-hosted deployments, need for transactional consistency, custom audit requirements

**Approach**: Store configuration as JSON in database:

```sql
CREATE TABLE ExtensionConfiguration (
    TenantId NVARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
    ExtensionId NVARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    ConfigurationJson NVARCHAR(MAX) NOT NULL,
    SchemaVersion INT NOT NULL,
    CreatedDate DATETIME2 NOT NULL,
    ModifiedDate DATETIME2 NOT NULL,
    ModifiedBy NVARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    CONSTRAINT PK_ExtensionConfiguration PRIMARY KEY (TenantId, ExtensionId)
);

CREATE TABLE ExtensionConfigurationHistory (
    Id BIGINT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    TenantId NVARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
    ExtensionId NVARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    ConfigurationJson NVARCHAR(MAX) NOT NULL,
    ChangeType NVARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,  -- Created, Updated, Deleted
    ChangedDate DATETIME2 NOT NULL,
    ChangedBy NVARCHAR(100) NOT NULL
);
```

**Trade-offs**:
- ✅ Transactional consistency with application data
- ✅ Custom audit trail implementation
- ✅ No external service dependencies
- ✅ Queryable configuration for reporting
- ❌ Manual cache management required
- ❌ Configuration changes require database updates

**Variation 4: Environment-Variable Overrides**

**When to Use**: Container deployments, Kubernetes environments, configuration per deployment slot

**Approach**: Allow environment variables to override stored configuration:

```csharp
public class EnvironmentOverrideConfigProvider : IExtensionConfigurationProvider
{
    private readonly IExtensionConfigurationProvider _baseProvider;

    public async Task<T> GetConfigurationAsync<T>(
        string tenantId,
        string extensionId) where T : class, new()
    {
        // Load base configuration
        var config = await _baseProvider.GetConfigurationAsync<T>(tenantId, extensionId);

        // Override with environment variables
        var envPrefix = $"EXT_{extensionId.ToUpper().Replace('-', '_')}__";

        foreach (var property in typeof(T).GetProperties())
        {
            var envVarName = $"{envPrefix}{property.Name.ToUpper()}";
            var envValue = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(envVarName);

            if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(envValue))
            {
                var convertedValue = Convert.ChangeType(envValue, property.PropertyType);
                property.SetValue(config, convertedValue);
            }
        }

        return config;
    }
}

// Environment variable example
// EXT_FL_PRIOR_AUTH__APIENDPOINT=https://test.api.florida.gov
// EXT_FL_PRIOR_AUTH__TIMEOUTSECONDS=60
```

**Trade-offs**:
- ✅ Easy per-environment configuration
- ✅ Kubernetes ConfigMap/Secret integration
- ✅ Container-friendly
- ❌ Environment variables limited to simple types
- ❌ Complex nested configuration difficult
- ❌ Not tenant-specific (applies to all tenants)

**Variation 5: Strongly-Typed Configuration Classes**

**When to Use**: Compile-time safety desired, IDE IntelliSense support, refactoring safety

**Approach**: Define C# classes for extension configuration:

```csharp
public class FloridaPriorAuthConfiguration
{
    [Required]
    [Url]
    public string ApiEndpoint { get; set; }

    [Required]
    [Secret]
    public string ApiKey { get; set; }

    [Range(1, 300)]
    public int TimeoutSeconds { get; set; } = 30;

    public bool EnableCaching { get; set; } = true;

    [MinLength(1)]
    public List<string> ServiceCodes { get; set; }
}

// Extension access with type safety
public async Task InitializeAsync(IExtensionContext context)
{
    var config = await context.Configuration
        .GetConfigurationAsync<FloridaPriorAuthConfiguration>(
            context.TenantId, this.Id);

    // Type-safe access with IntelliSense
    _apiClient = new ApiClient(config.ApiEndpoint, config.ApiKey);
    _timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(config.TimeoutSeconds);
}
```

**Trade-offs**:
- ✅ Compile-time type checking
- ✅ IntelliSense support
- ✅ Refactoring-safe
- ✅ Data annotations for validation
- ❌ Requires C# classes in shared library
- ❌ Schema changes require code deployment

**Variation 6: Schema-Free Configuration (JSON Document)**

**When to Use**: Highly dynamic extensions, schema evolves frequently, prototype stage

**Approach**: Store and retrieve configuration as dynamic JSON:

```csharp
public class DynamicExtensionConfigurationProvider : IExtensionConfigurationProvider
{
    public async Task<JsonDocument> GetConfigurationAsync(
        string tenantId,
        string extensionId)
    {
        var json = await _store.GetAsync(tenantId, extensionId);
        return JsonDocument.Parse(json);
    }
}

// Extension accesses configuration dynamically
public async Task InitializeAsync(IExtensionContext context)
{
    var configDoc = await context.Configuration
        .GetConfigurationAsync(context.TenantId, this.Id);

    var root = configDoc.RootElement;
    _apiEndpoint = root.GetProperty("apiEndpoint").GetString();
    _timeout = root.GetProperty("timeoutSeconds").GetInt32();
}
```

**Trade-offs**:
- ✅ Maximum flexibility
- ✅ No schema definition required
- ✅ Rapid iteration
- ❌ No compile-time validation
- ❌ Runtime errors for missing/wrong properties
- ❌ No IntelliSense support
- ❌ Difficult to refactor

